{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 171","reason":"The integers 7,14,21,28,35,42,49 occur in 45,38,31,24,17,10,3 of the factorials, respectively. Since 49 contributes two factors of 7, the exponent is 45+38+31+24+17+10+2×3=171.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2026_AMC_8_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factorial-valuations","label":"Sum factorial valuations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factorial-valuations":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"count-repeated-multiples","label":"Count how often each multiple of seven appears","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-repeated-multiples":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 77","reason":"Since 2010=2*3*5*67, the answer is the minimum of four valuations. They satisfy v2=1+...+12=78, v3=140, and v5>=89. Among the 79 multiples of 67, only quotients 71,73,79 introduce a larger prime, leaving 76 eligible multiples. The input 67^2 contributes one additional factor of 67, so v67=77 and the minimum is 77.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization"],"id":"restricted-prime-exponent-count","label":"Count the limiting prime exponent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"restricted-prime-exponent-count":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 0","reason":"15! contains three factors of 5 and at least three factors of 2, so 1000 divides 15!. It also divides 20!, hence their difference ends in at least three zeros and has hundreds digit 0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q02","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factorial-trailing-zeros","label":"Find three trailing zeros","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factorial-trailing-zeros":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:factorial-notation"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:factorial-notation"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, none of these; n=250","reason":"The exponent of 5 is floor(1005/5)+floor(1005/25)+floor(1005/125)+floor(1005/625)=201+40+8+1=250. The exponent of 2 is larger, so the largest n is 250, which is not listed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"count-factors-of-five","label":"Count factors of 5 in the factorial","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-factors-of-five":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 9","reason":"The product is 25^7 times 8^3=5^14 times 2^9. Exactly nine factors of 10 can be formed, so it ends in nine zeros.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_8_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_019"],"id":"pair-factors-of-ten","label":"Pair factors of 2 and 5","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-factors-of-ten":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:prime-factorization","cnm_019"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 492","reason":"There are always at least as many factors of 2 as of 5. For d = 5, 25, 125, 625, the sum of the three floor quotients is at least floor(2006/d)-2. These bounds total 399+78+14+1 = 492. The triple (624,624,758) attains all four bounds.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p050"],"id":"factor-count-bound-and-construction","label":"Bound the number of factors of five and attain the bound","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-count-bound-and-construction":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:extremal-principle","math:floor-ceiling"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:extremal-principle","math:floor-ceiling","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p050"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 8","reason":"Let z(m)=sum_{j>=1} floor(m/5^j). Checking n from 5 upward, z(2n)=3z(n) first holds for n=8,9,13,14, with k=1,1,2,2 respectively. Their sum is 44, whose digit sum is 8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"count-fives-in-order","label":"Count factors of five in consecutive cases","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-fives-in-order":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1/19","reason":"The exponent of 2 in 21! is 10 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 18. For every divisor of its odd part, there are 19 divisors obtained using exponents 0 through 18 of 2, and exactly one is odd. The probability is therefore 1/19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"odd-part-divisor-groups","label":"Group divisors by odd part","methodDirectNodeIds":{"odd-part-divisor-groups":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (12)","reason":"There are three factors of 5 and at least three factors of 2 in 19!, so H=0. Divisibility by 9 gives T+M in {3,12}; divisibility by 11 gives T-M in {-4,7}. The only valid digit pair is T=4, M=8, yielding T+M+H=12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factorial-digit-divisibility","label":"Use factorial divisibility","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factorial-digit-divisibility":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","cnm_090"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 23","reason":"Since 12!=2^10·3^5·5²·7·11, it has 11·6·3·2·2=792 positive divisors. Square divisors have even exponents, giving 6·3·2·1·1=36 choices. The probability is 36/792=1/22, so m+n=23.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-prime-exponents","label":"Count prime exponent choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponents":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_285"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (23)","reason":"2024 = 2^3·11·23. For n < 23, n! has no factor 23. At n = 23, the factorial contains 23, 11, and at least three factors of 2, so 23 is the least possible n.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10A_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-factors-in-factorial","label":"Locate the required prime factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-factors-in-factorial":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:prime-factorization","math:factorial-notation"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:prime-factorization","math:factorial-notation"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (r = 5)","reason":"The assignment count is [16!/((4!)^4·4!)]·12^4 = 16!/(2^4·4!). Therefore its exponent of 3 is (5 + 1) - 1 = 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"partition-roles-prime-factors","label":"Committees and factors of 3","methodDirectNodeIds":{"partition-roles-prime-factors":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_h234","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_h234","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 5","reason":"Choosing four chairs distinguishes the committees. Assigning secretaries and two ordinary members to each gives N = C(16,4)·12!/(2!)^4 = 16!/(4!·2^4). Thus v_3(N) = floor(16/3) + floor(16/9) - v_3(4!) = 5 + 1 - 1 = 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"partition-then-roles","label":"Form committees, then assign roles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"partition-then-roles":["cnm_h231","cnm_h234","math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h234","math:prime-valuations-factorials"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 8","reason":"In 15!, v_2=7+3+1=11, v_3=5+1=6, and v_5=3. Thus k=min(floor(11/2),6)=5 for base 12, while h=min(11,3)=3 for base 10. Their sum is 8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q33","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization","cnm_024"],"id":"count-prime-factors","label":"Count the factors that make trailing zeros","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-factors":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization","cnm_024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. k = 4","reason":"v_2(10!)=5+2+1=8 and v_3(10!)=3+1=4. Therefore the largest k with 12^k dividing 10! is min(floor(8/2),4)=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"prime-factor-count","label":"Count factors of the base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-factor-count":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","cnm_024"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","cnm_024","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 12","reason":"Let N = 90!/10²¹, since v5(90!) = 18 + 3 = 21. Also v2(90!) = 86, so N ≡ 0 modulo 4. Define U(m) as the product of integers up to m not divisible by 5. Modulo 25, U(90) = 1, U(18) = 4, and U(3) = 6. Thus N ≡ U(90)U(18)U(3)·2⁻²¹ ≡ 24·13 ≡ 12 modulo 25. Combining the two residues gives N ≡ 12 modulo 100.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"remove-zeros-and-find-remainders","label":"Remove the zeros and find small remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-zeros-and-find-remainders":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 12","reason":"The valuations are v₅(90!)=21 and v₂(90!)=86. Let U(N)=N!/5^{v₅(N!)}. Grouping factors not divisible by 5 gives U(90)≡1·4·6=24 modulo 25. Thus M=90!/10²¹ satisfies M≡24(2²¹)⁻¹≡12 modulo 25. Also v₂(M)=65, so M≡0 modulo 4. These conditions give M≡12 modulo 100.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"remove-fives-and-find-remainders","label":"Remove trailing zeros and track remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-fives-and-find-remainders":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (26)","reason":"98!+99!+100!=98!(1+99+99×100)=98!×10000. The exponent of 5 in 98! is 19+3=22, and 10000 contributes four more factors of 5, giving 26.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_8_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"factor-and-count-fives","label":"Factor the sum and count factors of five","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-count-fives":["cnm_p116","math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p116","math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 34","reason":"For prime n, the numerator has n-adic valuation n−1, while the denominator has valuation n, so it fails. The value n=4 also fails because v₂(15!)=11<12. Every composite n≥6 passes: for p^a exactly dividing n, the a one-unit deficits in the factorial valuation comparison are offset at the p^(a+1) term, since n/p≥a+1; the remaining differences are nonnegative. Including n=1, the count is 50−15 primes−1 exceptional composite=34.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factorial-divisibility-and-grouping","label":"Prime factors and equal groups","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factorial-divisibility-and-grouping":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (34)","reason":"Every prime p fails because v_p((p²-1)!)=p-1<p=v_p((p!)^p). Also, n=4 fails since the respective powers of 2 are 11 and 12. The ratio equals ((n-1)!/n) times the product of C(kn+n-1,n-1) for k=0,...,n-1. For every composite n>4, n divides (n-1)!, so this is integral; n=1 also works. Excluding the 15 primes and 4 leaves 50-15-1=34.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factorial-partition-count","label":"Prove divisibility by counting groups","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factorial-partition-count":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:double-counting","cnm_p157","cnm_h234"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 672","reason":"The product has factorization 2^30·3^13·5^5·7^3. A square divisor has 16, 7, 3, and 2 choices for those prime exponents, respectively, giving 16·7·3·2=672.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:factorial-notation","cnm_h231"],"id":"count-even-prime-exponents","label":"Count the even prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-even-prime-exponents":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","math:prime-factorization","math:factorial-notation","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 9","reason":"Every ten-term product contains at least eight factors of 2. Before a window contains 5^7=78125, it contains at most seven factors of 5. The first suitable window starts at k=78117 and has eight factors each of 2 and 5. After removing them, the result is odd and congruent to 4 modulo 5, so its last digit is 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1998_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p154","cnm_p156"],"id":"count-twos-and-fives","label":"Compare the factors of 2 and 5","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-twos-and-fives":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p154","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 1925 coins","reason":"Let m be the last share. Working backward gives amounts m*12^j/j! for 0<=j<=11. Since 11!=2^8*3^4*5^2*7*11, the initial amount being integral forces 1925 to divide m. Taking m=1925 makes every intermediate amount integral and positive. Since the initial amount is proportional to m, this also minimizes it.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"forward-prime-valuations","label":"Track prime exponents forward","methodDirectNodeIds":{"forward-prime-valuations":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 70","reason":"Write the factorial product as the product of k^(17-k) for k=2,...,16. Up to a square, this is 2*4*...*16=2^8*8!. Since 8!=2^7*3^2*5*7, exactly the primes 2, 5, and 7 need an additional factor. The least multiplier is 2*5*7=70.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"direct-prime-valuations","label":"Sum factorial prime valuations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"direct-prime-valuations":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-prime-exponents","label":"Count prime factors directly","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-exponents":["math:prime-factorization","math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:prime-valuations-factorials"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 1925 coins","reason":"Let m be the last share. Reversing j preceding divisions gives a remainder m·12^j/j!, so the initial amount is m·12^11/11!. Since 11! = 2^8·3^4·5^2·7·11, integrality of the initial amount forces m to be a multiple of 5^2·7·11 = 1925. Taking m = 1925 makes every reverse remainder integral, establishing both feasibility and minimality.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"backward-factorial-divisibility","label":"Work backward from the last share","methodDirectNodeIds":{"backward-factorial-divisibility":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1/19","reason":"The exponent of 2 in 21! is floor(21/2) + floor(21/4) + floor(21/8) + floor(21/16) = 10 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 18. An odd divisor uses exponent 0 rather than any of the 19 possible exponents of 2; all other exponent choices cancel, giving 1/19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"count-two-exponent-choices","label":"Count the choices for the power of two","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-two-exponent-choices":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h137"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:divisor-count-and-sum","cnm_h137"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (8)","reason":"The factorization is 12!=2^10×3^5×5^2×7×11. Its largest square divisor is 2^10×3^4×5^2, whose square root is 2^5×3^2×5. The requested exponent sum is 5+2+1=8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"count-prime-factors-and-pair","label":"Count prime factors and keep pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-prime-factors-and-pair":["math:prime-factorization","math:prime-valuations-factorials"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:prime-valuations-factorials","status":"mapped","total":27}
